WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:08.100
A slice of pizza simply melts in our mouth.

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:12.130
A bite of the delicious burger is
never a “no-no”.

00:00:12.130 --> 00:00:14.030
And what about desserts?

00:00:14.030 --> 00:00:19.530
A piece of cake or a brownie is
always at the top of our wish list!

00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:24.270
Whatever food we eat, it always has to pass
that churning process or the

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:26.240
physical break down!

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:29.400
Mastication, that is chewing, is one of the
most

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:32.840
important commencing steps of digestion.

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:35.270
And who helps in this process?

00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:39.090
The
sole components of the buccal cavity involved

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:42.220
in mastication are the TEETH!

00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:45.590
They help in biting, chewing and grinding
the food we eat.

00:00:45.590 --> 00:00:50.870
Let us have a look at the “Teeth” in detail
in this video.

00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:53.910
To understand teeth,
let us begin with knowing the structure of

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:56.440
a typical tooth first.

00:00:56.440 --> 00:00:59.810
A tooth consists of two main regions.

00:00:59.810 --> 00:01:04.019
One which is above the gum surface and
the other is present below it.

00:01:04.019 --> 00:01:06.220
What are gums by the way?

00:01:06.220 --> 00:01:10.460
Gums, also called the
“gingiva” are the soft tissue linings

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:12.400
in the mouth.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:15.270
But gums appear to be so
hard.

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:18.439
So how can they be called soft tissue linings?

00:01:18.439 --> 00:01:23.409
Well that is because gums
are soft tissues, but they are tightly bound

00:01:23.409 --> 00:01:26.390
to the bones under them.

00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:29.700
They act as the anchoring point for our teeth.

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:33.510
They help in firmly holding the
teeth at their position.

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:36.450
In humans for example, the teeth are firmly
rooted in

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:38.659
the sockets formed in the gums.

00:01:38.659 --> 00:01:43.189
This morphology gets a name “Thecodont”.

00:01:43.189 --> 00:01:46.999
This simply means the type in which teeth
are embedded in the sockets

00:01:46.999 --> 00:01:49.170
formed in the gums.

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:54.200
Now after having a glance at the gums, let’s
look at the actual tooth!

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:56.450
This is
what a typical tooth in a vertical section

00:01:56.450 --> 00:01:57.450
looks like!

00:01:57.450 --> 00:02:00.079
Let us begin from the top!

00:02:00.079 --> 00:02:03.719
Here, this part, seen above the gums is called
as the

00:02:03.719 --> 00:02:04.719
crown!

00:02:04.719 --> 00:02:09.440
So the part which we can see is the crown
of our tooth.

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:13.760
Since the crown is the
part exposed to all types of food particles,

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:18.069
and which encounters microbes as
well, it needs to be well protected!

00:02:18.069 --> 00:02:23.390
Hence, it is coated with an extremely hard
covering named ENAMEL.

00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:27.470
Now if I ask you, what is stronger, a bone,
or the enamel?

00:02:27.470 --> 00:02:30.819
I am sure your
answer will be Bone!

00:02:30.819 --> 00:02:32.000
But that is not the case!

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:34.780
It is actually the enamel!

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:35.810
Yes!

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:38.491
Enamel is the hardest tissue or substance
in the human body!

00:02:38.491 --> 00:02:44.780
The next layer after enamel is also extremely
hard.

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:46.629
This layer is what we are
talking about!

00:02:46.629 --> 00:02:49.959
It is called the Dentine.

00:02:49.959 --> 00:02:50.959
What do you think would be its

00:02:50.959 --> 00:02:51.959
function?

00:02:51.959 --> 00:02:56.549
As we can see here, the dentine mainly acts
as a connecting bridge

00:02:56.549 --> 00:03:00.459
between the crown on top and this root lying
below it.

00:03:00.459 --> 00:03:01.459
Root?

00:03:01.459 --> 00:03:04.200
Do teeth also have roots?

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:06.269
Yes they do!

00:03:06.269 --> 00:03:10.010
This region is referred to as the
root of this tooth.

00:03:10.010 --> 00:03:13.120
Just like plant roots, these roots help in
anchoring the tooth

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:15.310
firmly in the socket of gums.

00:03:15.310 --> 00:03:19.709
Now let us talk about the next part… this
one!

00:03:19.709 --> 00:03:21.500
Do you know what this is called
as?

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:24.300
It is called the pulp!

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:25.640
And why so?

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:30.480
Well, the portion is extremely soft and
fleshy, and is richly supplied with blood

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:32.409
vessels and nerves.

00:03:32.409 --> 00:03:35.580
So whenever we
feel the pain in our tooth, it is this region

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:37.609
that sends us a signal!

00:03:37.609 --> 00:03:39.090
So is that its sole function?

00:03:39.090 --> 00:03:41.640
Of course not!

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:45.260
The blood vessels in the pulp help in
nourishing the tooth.

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:49.390
Now, are you able to locate this part?

00:03:49.390 --> 00:03:50.969
This is called the Cementum.

00:03:50.969 --> 00:03:54.109
Isn’t the
name self-explanatory?

00:03:54.109 --> 00:03:55.150
Yes!

00:03:55.150 --> 00:03:57.859
It acts like a tough layer that covers the
complete

00:03:57.859 --> 00:03:58.859
root.

00:03:58.859 --> 00:04:03.239
As you can notice here, the structure widely
spreads across the complete

00:04:03.239 --> 00:04:05.420
root of the tooth.

00:04:05.420 --> 00:04:08.689
So this is the structure of a typical tooth!

00:04:08.689 --> 00:04:13.470
The crown is this top most region that
is usually visible to us.

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:16.280
It consists of an extremely hard protective
layer called

00:04:16.280 --> 00:04:17.280
the Enamel.

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:20.269
Below this, is the dentine.

00:04:20.269 --> 00:04:25.110
And just below the dentine comes the
root which has this fleshy pulp, richly supplied

00:04:25.110 --> 00:04:28.030
with blood vessels and nerves.

00:04:28.030 --> 00:04:32.820
This is one more layer named Cementum that
covers the root from below.

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:35.390
And
this root is embedded in this jaw bone as

00:04:35.390 --> 00:04:37.720
we can see here.

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:40.040
This bone is covered
on top with the gum.

00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:44.070
So this is how the complete tooth appears
to us in a cross

00:04:44.070 --> 00:04:45.750
section.

00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:48.130
So are we done learning about the teeth?

00:04:48.130 --> 00:04:49.130
Of course not!

00:04:49.130 --> 00:04:53.880
There are several
amazing concepts yet to be covered.

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:58.940
Let us meet in the next video and cover
these concepts!

